     3,
     "<div><br></div><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"auto\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Friday, March 29, 2024 at 8:05:57 AM UTC-7 <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">kien...@gmail.com</a> wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div>Hello everyone, <br></div><div><br></div><div>I have a RI-CLPM (RICLPM5 from <a href=\"https://jeroendmulder.github.io/RI-CLPM/lavaan.html\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://jeroendmulder.github.io/RI-CLPM/lavaan.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792414739770000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3m2d0Bt-EehsFqgOM_kdwP\">Moulder &amp; Hamaker</a>) with four measurement occasions and constraints over time. I have to compute 2*9 models where x-parameters always have the same scale, but the scales of the y-parameters are different from x-parameters and differ from the first 9 models to the second 9 models. <br></div><div>Hence, I am interested in having not only constrained cross-lagged Estimates, but them also standardized. <br></div><div><br></div><div>The &quot;Estimates&quot; are nicely equivalent, but the Std.all-values differ having &quot;standardized=T&quot; in the summary-function, e.g.:</div><div><br></div><div>\r\n\r\n<span aria-label=\"Console Output\" role=\"document\" style=\"font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;,monospace;outline:none;border:medium;word-break:break-all;margin:0px;white-space:pre-wrap;line-height:1.2;color:rgb(0,0,0);font-style:normal;font-variant-ligatures:normal;font-variant-caps:normal;font-weight:400;letter-spacing:normal;text-indent:0px;text-transform:none;word-spacing:0px;background-color:rgb(255,255,255);text-decoration-style:initial;text-decoration-color:initial\"><span role=\"document\" style=\"outline:none\"><span style=\"outline:none;border:medium;word-break:break-all;margin:0px;white-space:pre-wrap\"><font size=\"2\">Regressions:\r\n            